  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM EDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\n- Low clouds and light rain from remnants of Isaias today\n\n Post tropical cyclone Isaias will bring heavy rainfall through \nthe southern Appalachians and move through the Mid Atlantic today.\nSoutheast Michigan remains on the edge of the tropical moisture\nand will bring some low clouds to regions south of I 96 and west\nof the US 131 today. Best chance for rain will be through the\neast of I 69. Overall rainfall will be fairly light. \n\n- Warm air continues through Tuesday; Rain chances Tue and Wed\n\n The warm air conveyor belt will continue through Tuesday. Max\ntemperatures through this time frame will be 10 to 20 degrees\nabove normal. Monday's Max temps are expected to be in the upper \n70s to low 80s across lower Michigan. These temps are associated \nwith a positively tilted upper level ridge, which should keep any\nshowers north of the lower peninsula through Monday. That ridge \nwill break down Monday night into Tuesday. so expect a weak upper \nlevel trough to swing a mid level system through the area into \nTuesday. QPF is around 0.25 at this time.\n\n The pattern flattens out Tuesday into Wednesday with a more \nzonal trend which could keep the best rainfall on Tuesday north \nof US 50. However,the EC ensemble PWATS for that band through \nnorthern lower MI is around 1.5 inches. If that pool of moisture \nshifts to the south then locally higher amounts of rainfall could\nbe possible. Lows will be in the mid 60s by Tuesday morning, \npeaking into the upper 70s Tuesday afternoon. So a warm, rain for \nthe most part.\n\n Depending where the moisture band sets up on Wednesday, the \nsouthern half of lower Michigan could see some decent rainfall \nWednesday, though currently the best PWATS are south of the I 94 \ncorridor. \n \n- Chance for showers and storms late this week\n\n A pattern shift is ahead, though latest mid range guidance\npushes the pattern change to the end of the work week. Any rain \nand clouds will temper temperatures through Midweek. Max temps \nshould remain above normal through Thursday. A cold front late\nthis week will drop temperatures below normal, bringing more\nAutumn-esque temperatures. \n\n The guidance continues to become out of phase beyond Thursday. \nThe GFS brings a deepening synoptic system through the area \nFriday. The EC has a much shallower solution, with any moisture \nbands bifurcating the area. The NAEFS and EC ensembles are \nsimilar, which gives more credence that the northern portion of \nthe system remains through the Upper Great Lakes, and a vort max \nassociated with the southern portion of the system skirting \nthrough IN and Ohio. That should mean any rain chances Friday \nshould be south of the I 96 corridor, and heaviest rain south of \nMichigan.",
